{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Ethan, the pertinent information is: sensory: auditory fatigue; medical: device care, not an emergency issue; social: access to informal peer conversation; and behaviour: may appear disengaged when access is missing. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: overlapping talk and background noise; teachers speaking while facing away; uncaptioned media; and listening fatigue late in the day. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Ethan, the most useful EA actions are to secure captions and interpreter sight lines; repeat peer comments, not only teacher speech; provide quiet work options; and check comprehension privately. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-F-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.475, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.4875, "end": 1.7625, "start_char": 22, "end_char": 26